WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.128
Hi, I'm Gusset!

00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:05.963
How SGLang avoids recomputing old conversations.

00:00:09.808 --> 00:00:12.328
The idea: do not throw an evicted prefix away.

00:00:12.328 --> 00:00:14.568
Keep it lower down, and load it back.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.191
The radix tree finds the longest prefix it has seen.

00:00:21.611 --> 00:00:25.434
If that prefix is still in GPU memory, it is reused at once.

00:00:25.854 --> 00:00:29.658
With write through, each new prefix is copied down to CPU memory.

00:00:30.078 --> 00:00:32.962
Then to shared storage, keyed by a hash of its tokens.

00:00:33.382 --> 00:00:37.935
On a storage hit, pages are prefetched to CPU, then loaded onto the GPU.

00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:42.495
On the GPU, the cache is grouped by layer, for attention.

00:00:42.915 --> 00:00:46.746
In CPU memory it is grouped by page, so a page is one write.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.934
What if a request is ready before its prefetch finishes?

00:00:51.354 --> 00:00:53.933
Best effort starts now and recomputes the rest.

00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:56.338
Wait complete waits for the whole prefix.

00:00:56.758 --> 00:00:59.566
Timeout waits up to a limit that grows with the prefix.

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:04.400
Attention keeps a row per token, so any prefix is valid.

00:01:04.907 --> 00:01:08.023
A Mamba state is valid only at a checkpoint, so each checkpoint

00:01:08.023 --> 00:01:09.581
is backed up on its own.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.024
Here is the formula.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.476
In round r of the benchmark, all but the new question was seen before.

00:01:16.896 --> 00:01:18.469
Divide by the whole prompt.

00:01:18.469 --> 00:01:21.614
By round fifty five, that ceiling is ninety eight percent.

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:27.153
GPU only falls off a cliff, then GPU plus CPU.

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:28.627
Storage rides the ceiling.

00:01:29.047 --> 00:01:31.546
Effective throughput counts cached tokens as served.

00:01:31.546 --> 00:01:34.044
That is the fifteen point five times.

00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:38.724
Storage lets the hit rate reach the reuse that exists.

00:01:38.724 --> 00:01:41.156
The workload decides how much reuse that is.

00:01:43.483 --> 00:01:46.747
One tree, three tiers, and a hit rate the workload sets.

00:01:46.747 --> 00:01:47.043
Bye!

00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:50.175
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:50.175 --> 00:01:50.773
I'm Gusset.

00:01:50.773 --> 00:01:51.072
Bye!
